<mujoco model="tong">
  <!-- A spring-loaded tong: two jaws on one hinge, held open by a torsion
       spring, closing only while something pushes them shut.

       AN ALUMINIUM TOOL, and the thickness is a simulation requirement as
       much as a design choice. The jaws began 6 mm thick at 30 g. The YAM
       gripper saturates at 38 N, which on a 30 g jaw is 1268 m/s^2: in one
       5 ms step that is 15.9 mm of unopposed travel, against a jaw 6 mm
       thick. The contact cannot hold it — MuJoCo's soft-constraint time
       constant is floored at 2*dt, which for 30 g is about 300 N/m and puts
       the equilibrium penetration at 127 mm — so the finger went THROUGH
       the jaw, the contacts vanished, and the energy stored in the
       penetration came out as 400 rad/s of spin and a NaN two steps later.
       Measured, on one environment in 8192, about once every 20 million
       steps.

       Not fixable by tuning contacts: holding the penetration under a
       millimetre needs 38 kN/m, which is a time constant of 0.9 ms against
       a floor of 10. The dials are force, mass and thickness, and the
       honest one is the tool. 120 x 12 x 30 mm of aluminium at 2700 kg/m^3
       is 116.6 g — the mass follows from the material and the size rather
       than being chosen — which puts the penetration near 3 mm inside a
       jaw whose half-thickness is 6.

       The lift task never met this with the same gripper and a 50 g cube:
       9.5 mm of travel per step cannot cross a 40 mm cube.

       THE HINGE ZERO IS THE OPEN POSITION, and that is a portability
       requirement rather than a style choice. MuJoCo and Newton let a
       joint spring rest at a stated angle (`springref`), but Genesis has
       no such concept — its passive joint force is `-stiffness * q`
       (`genesis/engine/solvers/rigid/abd/forward_dynamics.py:985`), so
       its spring always rests at zero. Defining "fully open" as q = 0
       makes all three agree exactly, with no springref anywhere.

       The travel runs from -0.15 rad to 0.3244, and the open END of that
       is NOT where the tong sits. A spring tong's open position is set by
       its spring; the only place the jaws physically stop each other is
       closed, where the pads meet. Giving the spring's rest position a
       hard limit in the same place puts two constraints in permanent
       disagreement over one point. The extra travel is headroom the
       spring never uses.

       Measured on this asset, not estimated:

         q = 0.0000 rad  fully open, 31.71 mm between the pads
         q = 0.0695 rad  closed onto a 25 mm object
         q = 0.3244 rad  pads meet (0.89 mm past touching, the joint stop)
         q = 0.5456 rad  the jaw frames align; unreachable, past the stop

       The jaw's body frame is pre-rotated by that last angle about -z, so
       that inside it the jaw runs along +x exactly like the base one: the
       inertia is then diagonal in the body frame and identical for both,
       which is one less thing that can differ between backends. Turning
       the joint positive rotates the jaw back down, i.e. CLOSES it.

       The spring is scaled with the tool. Making the jaws aluminium
       multiplies the hinge's inertia about the pivot by 3.895, so
       stiffness and damping are multiplied by the same factor: the
       natural frequency stays at 58.907 rad/s and the damping ratio at
       0.4713, both unchanged from the 6 mm tong. The tool is heavier and
       thicker and behaves identically.

       Both jaws carry an explicit <inertial>: left to a solver's own
       geom-derived estimate, the three would not necessarily agree, and
       an inertia mismatch shows up as a different swing rate rather than
       as an error. It describes the 120 x 12 x 30 mm bar; the 16 mm pad
       at its tip is not counted, as it was not before. The mass is stated
       once here and every backend reads it. -->
  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <!-- Every geom states type="box" itself, rather than inheriting it from
       the class below. MuJoCo resolves childclass through nested bodies
       and Genesis does not: it loaded the two boxes on tong_base as
       boxes, and the two on tong_jaw — one body deeper — as a single
       non-convex MESH of 502 vertices. Against the bench that mesh
       produced forty contacts an environment where each box produced
       five, and the step cost four times what the same scene carrying a
       cube costs. Stating the type is one word per geom and removes the
       question. -->
  <default>
    <default class="tong">
      <geom type="box" rgba="0.72 0.74 0.78 1" friction="0.9 0.005 0.0001"
            contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="3"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- Lower jaw, and the body the tong is carried by. Runs along +x
         from the pivot at the origin; its top face is the z = 0 plane. -->
    <body name="tong_base" pos="0 0 0" childclass="tong">
      <freejoint name="tong_free"/>
      <inertial pos="0.06 0 0" mass="0.11664"
                diaginertia="1.01477e-05 1.48716e-04 1.41368e-04"/>
      <geom name="tong_base_arm" type="box" size="0.06 0.006 0.015" pos="0.06 0.006 0"/>
      <geom name="tong_base_pad" type="box" size="0.008 0.006 0.015" pos="0.112 -0.006 0"/>
      <!-- Where the robot is meant to hold the tool: midway between the
           two jaws, 50 mm out from the pivot. A task aiming a gripper at
           the tong needs a point to aim AT, and its frame origin is the
           wrong one twice over — the origin is the pivot, where the jaws
           already touch so closing on it does nothing, and lying on a
           bench the pivot is half a jaw's width off the surface, so a
           gripper sent there goes through the bench.

           50 mm rather than the 60 the 6 mm tong used. Across both jaws
           the tool is 56.39 mm wide there and 62.46 mm at 60, against a
           gripper that opens to 74.65: the nearer point leaves 18.26 mm
           of approach clearance where the further one leaves 12.19, and
           still holds the tool 70 mm from its tip, which is the leverage
           that works it. -->
      <site name="grip_site" pos="0.05 -0.016196 0" size="0.004" rgba="1 0.5 0 1"/>

      <body name="tong_jaw" pos="0 0 0" quat="0.963019 0 0 -0.269434">
        <joint name="tong_hinge" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" range="-0.15 0.3244"
               stiffness="1.9476" damping="0.03116"/>
        <inertial pos="0.06 0 0" mass="0.11664"
                  diaginertia="1.01477e-05 1.48716e-04 1.41368e-04"/>
        <geom name="tong_jaw_arm" type="box" size="0.06 0.006 0.015" pos="0.06 -0.006 0"/>
        <geom name="tong_jaw_pad" type="box" size="0.008 0.006 0.015" pos="0.112 0.006 0"/>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- The two jaws overlap at their shared pivot, as the links of any
       hinge do. MuJoCo filters that pair for being parent and child, so
       a standalone tong behaves; loaded as one entity among several in a
       scene it does not, and the pair starts colliding. The contact then
       pushes the jaws APART, which drives the hinge past its own open
       stop and holds it there: measured at -0.12 rad on mjlab and
       Newton against +0.033 on Genesis, whose scene disables
       self-collision and so never saw it.

       Excluded explicitly, so the answer depends neither on a backend's
       reimplementation of the parent rule nor on whether whoever builds
       the scene remembered to turn self-collision off. -->
  <contact>
    <exclude body1="tong_base" body2="tong_jaw"/>
  </contact>
</mujoco>
